This study presents a measurement of the differential decay rate for the process $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ as a function of the $q^2$ variable, representing the squared momentum of the $\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ system. Utilizing proton-proton collision data collected at a center-of-mass energy of $\sqrt{s}=13$ TeV, with an integrated luminosity of 3.0 fb$^{-1}$, the analysis was performed using the LHCb detector. The results were derived from a sample of $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ candidates, reconstructed via the decay chain $D_s^{-}ightarrow K^{+}\pi^{-}\pi^{-}$, and were obtained by comparing the signal against the $B^0ightarrow D^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ process, where the $D^{-}$ meson is identified through the $K^{+}\pi^{-}$ final state. The analysis incorporates a model-independent approach, employing $z$ variables to parameterize the $q^2$ dependence. The findings indicate that the differential decay rate follows a $(1-y)^{2}(1+2y)$ behavior, where $y$ denotes the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ helicity
